{"t_ms":8314,"dir":"in","stream":"session:20261011_14","frame":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"20261011_14","update":{"_meta":{"goose":{"created":1791678630,"messageId":"msg_vrtx_011CfuWHburRM9DjbF4WqsLE","toolCall":{"extensionName":"developer","toolName":"write"}}},"rawInput":{"content":"# Understanding Tides\n\n## What Are Tides?\n\nTides are the periodic rise and fall of sea levels caused primarily by the gravitational pull of the Moon and the Sun on Earth's oceans. This natural phenomenon has fascinated humans for millennia and continues to play a crucial role in marine ecosystems, navigation, and coastal planning.\n\n## The Lunar Connection\n\nThe Moon is the dominant force driving tidal patterns. As the Moon orbits Earth, its gravity pulls on our planet's water bodies, creating bulges on the side of Earth facing the Moon and on the opposite side. This results in two high tides and two low tides approximately every 24 hours and 50 minutes, a period known as the tidal day.\n\n## The Sun's Role\n\nWhile the Sun is much larger than the Moon, its greater distance from Earth makes its tidal effect about half as strong as the Moon's influence. However, when the Sun, Earth, and Moon align—during new and full moons—their combined gravitational effects create especially pronounced tides called spring tides. Conversely, when they form a right angle, we experience weaker neap tides.\n\n## Types of Tides\n\nThere are generally three tidal patterns observed around the world. Semidiurnal tides occur twice daily and are common along the Atlantic Coast. Diurnal tides happen once per day and are found in parts of the Gulf of Mexico and Southeast Asia. Mixed tides combine characteristics of both and appear in regions like the Pacific Coast.\n\n## Tidal Ranges\n\nThe difference between high and low tide is called the tidal range. This varies significantly depending on location, coastal geography, and lunar cycles. Some coastal areas experience dramatic tidal ranges exceeding 40 feet, while others have minimal variations of just a few inches. The Bay of Fundy in Canada has the world's largest tidal range.\n\n## Practical Importance\n\nTides are essential for shipping routes, as ports must be navigated during optimal water levels. Fishing communities have relied on tidal knowledge for generations. Additionally, tidal energy is increasingly being harnessed as a renewable energy source in various locations worldwide.\n\n## Tidal Characteristics by Location\n\n| Location | Average Range (ft) | Tidal Type | Notable Feature |\n|----------|-------------------|-----------|-----------------|\n| Bay of Fundy, Canada | 45.0 | Semidiurnal | Largest in world |\n| English Channel | 25.0 | Semidiurnal | Predictable pattern |\n| Gulf of Mexico | 1.5 | Diurnal | Minimal variation |\n| Pacific Northwest | 12.0 | Mixed | Complex pattern |\n| Mediterranean Sea | 1.2 | Semidiurnal | Smallest recorded |\n\n## Conclusion\n\nUnderstanding tides requires knowledge of astronomy, physics, and oceanography. These rhythmic movements of water continue to shape our coasts, influence our oceans, and remind us of the intricate gravitational dance between Earth, Moon, and Sun. As we face rising sea levels and climate change, tidal science becomes increasingly important for coastal communities worldwide.\n","path":"/tmp/nix-shell.gmTW3Y/.tmpFbtKES/tides.md"},"sessionUpdate":"tool_call","title":"write · /tmp/nix-shell.gmTW3Y/.tmpFbtKES/tides.md","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01TkTCjJNTBX7CchbUxRLHzV"}}}}
